window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-a743f22d0f545b97"]={"key":"ny_main-a743f22d0f545b97","group":"ny_main","uid":"a743f22d0f545b97","rank":83,"title":"temporary coating cell operating procedure decision","occupation":"production_worker_supervisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You’re Alia Penrose, the first-line supervisor for Candlewick Manufacturing’s component-coating cell. For the temporary night run on 8 July 2025, Eastharbour Mutual is expecting 480 coated components between 22:00 and 06:00. The cell has had three recurring temperature trips in the last two shifts, and maintenance has not confirmed whether an operator may reset one; Emeka Lombardi has nevertheless promised Eastharbour Mutual that the run will continue without a maintenance call-out. Mateo Crowther and Bekele Wolstenholme will be on the line, and Oona Achterberg needs an approval-ready document she can hand to a broad, non-specialist customer audience. Write `temporary_coating_cell_operating_procedure.pptx` and put it in the top level of the working directory. Make it a practical training presentation of no more than eight slides, in plain language, covering how the shift starts, runs, stops, records what happened, and escalates when the procedure cannot be completed, under UK rules as at 8 July 2025. The pressure to clear a trip and carry on is already being discussed, so deal with that common misuse directly. Make one clear operating recommendation for this run, supported by the facts, keep the 480-component figure consistent wherever it appears, and finish on that decision rather than a list of next steps. Do not include software click-throughs or invented legal thresholds.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `temporary_coating_cell_operating_procedure.pptx` — An approval-ready, audience-specific training presentation containing the temporary component-coating-cell operating procedure and one bounded recommendation for the 8 July 2025 night run. (Use exactly this filename.; Place the file at the top level of the solver's starting working directory.; Use no more than eight slides.; Write for operators, supervisors, and broad non-specialist customer stakeholders.; Use plain language and make the procedure usable without asking the author a question.; Cover normal operation, stop-work conditions, emergency response, incomplete-procedure handling, escalation, records, and the final operating decision.; Apply UK requirements as at 8 July 2025 without inventing legal thresholds or unsupported site-specific facts.; Do not include software click-through instructions.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/temporary_coating_cell_operating_procedure.pptx","bytes":53844,"extension":".pptx","url":"assets/78d831485861367ae96136ccf9fe60284cbabda740bae6d4dd885e760ae29f90.pptx","sha256":"78d831485861367ae96136ccf9fe60284cbabda740bae6d4dd885e760ae29f90","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"=== 幻灯片 1 ===\nCANDLEWICK MANUFACTURING | APPROVAL-READY TRAINING BRIEF\nTemporary coating-cell\noperating procedure\nTemporary night run | 8 July 2025 | UK\nFor operators, supervisors and non-specialist customer stakeholders\nRUN SCOPE\n480\ncoated components\n22:00 to 06:00 | one temporary night run\nCustomer expectation: Eastharbour Mutual\nTHE OPERATING BOUNDARY\n0\noperator resets\nwhile authority is unresolved\n!\nany trip or unsafe\ncondition: safe stop + escalate\nR\nrestart only after\ncompetent authorized release\nContinuity promises do not override safeguarding, isolation, exposure control or stop-work duties.\nDOCUMENT CONTROL\nOwner: Alia Penrose, first-line supervisor  |  Status: approval required before use\nProposed effective date: 8 July 2025  |  Rev 0.1  |  Approver: production manager + competent maintenance and quality roles\nReview / expiry: before the next shift, or after any trip, change, incident or maintenance confirmation.\nCANDLEWICK MANUFACTURING | COMPONENT-COATING CELL\nTemporary arrangement | UK | 8 July 2025\n1/8\n\n=== 幻灯片 2 ===\n01 / BEFORE START\nBrief the shift. Verify the cell.\nThe temporary boundary must be understood before anyone starts the coating process.\nSHIFT BRIEF / HANDOVER\n- State the temporary status, the 480-component scope and the 22:00 to 06:00 window.\n- Share the three recurring temperature trips in the last two shifts; treat any further trip as an escalation event.\n- Name the operator, supervisor, maintenance, quality and emergency or incident-reporting roles.\n- Rehearse the expectation: stop, protect people, hold affected work and escalate when a step cannot be completed.\n- Only competent, trained and currently authorized personnel may operate; the supervisor verifies training or sign-off.\nPRE-START CHECK\n- Work area is clear; access and egress are safe.\n- Equipment condition is acceptable; no visible damage, leak or uncontrolled residue.\n- Guards and interlocks are present and effective; do not start with a failed safeguard.\n- Controls and emergency-stop arrangements are available and checked as the site procedure permits.\n- Required utilities or services, ventilation and other exposure controls are available.\n- Current risk assessment, approved recipe, equipment instructions, COSHH assessment, SDS and emergency arrangements are available.\nPPE follows the site's COSHH assessment and current safety data. PPE complements engineering controls; it never replaces guarding or ventilation.\nIf a check is missing, unclear or cannot be completed: do not start. Make safe, prevent use, record the gap and escalate.\nCANDLEWICK MANUFACTURING | COMPONENT-COATING CELL\nTemporary arrangement | UK | 8 July 2025\n2/8\n\n=== 幻灯片 3 ===\n02 / START + RUN\nStay inside the approved operating envelope.\nUse the controlled recipe and equipment information available to the competent team.\n1\nLOAD THE APPROVED SETUP\nUse the approved recipe, setpoints, material, equipment configuration and operating instructions. Do not improvise a parameter or workaround.\n2\nCHECK CONTROLS ARE LIVE\nStart only with guards and interlocks in place and effective, ventilation or other exposure controls available, and PPE selected from the COSHH assessment and SDS.\n3\nRUN AND WATCH\nMonitor the relevant temperature and process indicators throughout the run. Record abnormal readings, alarms, stops and actions as they occur.\n4\nPAUSE WHEN UNCLEAR\nIf a reading, alarm or process condition cannot be established against approved information, stop safely and call the supervisor. Do not experiment.\nOPERATORS DO NOT\n- bypass, defeat or bridge a guard or interlock\n- operate with a failed safeguard\n- repair, adjust or change a protective or control system\n- clear a dangerous obstruction without authorized isolation\n- release product or invent a setpoint\nSTOPPING IS NOT ISOLATION\nMaintenance intervention requires competent authorized isolation / lockout or tagout.\nNormal operation is allowed only with the approved configuration and current controlled information. A customer deadline does not authorize a change to that envelope.\nCANDLEWICK MANUFACTURING | COMPONENT-COATING CELL\nTemporary arrangement | UK | 8 July 2025\n3/8\n\n=== 幻灯片 4 ===\n03 / TEMPERATURE TRIP\nOne trip is a stop-and-escalate event.\nThe recurring pattern is unresolved. Do not turn an alarm into permission to carry on.\n0\nOPERATOR RESETS WHILE MAINTENANCE AUTHORITY IS UNRESOLVED\nNo repeat attempts\nto keep production moving.\n1\nSAFE STOP\nUse the normal stop or emergency stop as appropriate. Keep people clear. Do not reach in, defeat a safeguard or restart.\n>\n2\nPRESERVE FACTS\nNotify the supervisor. Record time, material or batch ID, quantity in process, temperature or alarm indication, preceding conditions and actions taken.\n>\n3\nAUTHORIZED RETURN\nMaintenance controls investigation, repair, fault reset and protective/control changes under applicable isolation and authorization. Restart needs an identified cause or safe disposition, required checks and competent authorized release.\nThree trips in the last two shifts make recurrence an escalation event. Emeka Lombardi's continuity promise to Eastharbour Mutual does not grant reset authority or waive a safe stop.\nIf maintenance has not confirmed operator authority, the boundary remains 0 operator resets.\nCANDLEWICK MANUFACTURING | COMPONENT-COATING CELL\nTemporary arrangement | UK | 8 July 2025\n4/8\n\n=== 幻灯片 5 ===\n04 / STOP WORK + EMERGENCY\nWhen in doubt, make the cell safe.\nStop first. Protect people. Prevent an unauthorized restart. Escalate through site arrangements.\nSTOP  |  PROTECT PEOPLE  |  PREVENT RESTART  |  ESCALATE\nSTOP WORK IF...\n- a guard or interlock is missing, failed or ineffective\n- temperature is uncontrolled or unexplained\n- ventilation or another required exposure control is unavailable\n- there is a leak, spill or uncontrolled coating residue\n- access or egress is unsafe\n- an alarm's meaning cannot be established\n- an injury, near miss or other event makes continued operation unsafe\nEMERGENCY RESPONSE\n1\nUse the site's emergency arrangements; raise the alarm or summon emergency help as appropriate.\n2\nMove to safety, evacuate or give first aid only within training and site arrangements.\n3\nDo not troubleshoot a life-safety event. Do not re-enter or restart until authorized.\nAfter any incident: notify, preserve facts where safe, and hold affected work.\nREPORTING\nInternal recording and escalation are required. The responsible competent function checks whether RIDDOR 2013 reporting applies; not every trip is automatically RIDDOR-reportable.\nCANDLEWICK MANUFACTURING | COMPONENT-COATING CELL\nTemporary arrangement | UK | 8 July 2025\n5/8\n\n=== 幻灯片 6 ===\n05 / INCOMPLETE PROCEDURE\nNo workaround. Use the safe-hold path.\nA missing check, unclear alarm or unavailable control is a decision point, not an invitation to improvise.\n1\nSAFE STATE\nStop and leave the cell safe. Isolate hazardous energy when the task requires it.\n>\n2\nCONTROL ACCESS\nPrevent unauthorized use. Apply status, lock or tag arrangements only as authorized by the site.\n>\n3\nHOLD WORK\nIdentify and hold or quarantine affected components from the abnormal or incomplete run.\n>\n4\nRECORD THE GAP\nRecord the missing step, time, condition, trip or alarm, material and quantity, plus isolation status.\n>\n5\nESCALATE FOR A DECISION\nSupervisor receives the report; maintenance controls equipment intervention; quality controls product disposition; the production or customer lead receives factual impact.\nNO UNAUTHORIZED CONTINUATION\nIf a required step cannot be completed, the cell remains safely stopped until an authorized decision and release exist.\nCUSTOMER UPDATE\nReport status, safety-related stoppage, affected work and uncertainty factually. Do not promise continued operation.\nStopping a machine is not isolation; maintenance or entry into a hazardous area requires competent authorized isolation / lockout or tagout.\nCANDLEWICK MANUFACTURING | COMPONENT-COATING CELL\nTemporary arrangement | UK | 8 July 2025\n6/8\n\n=== 幻灯片 7 ===\n06 / RECORD + HANDOVER\nMake the run reconstructable.\nRecord what was planned, what happened and what remains under control at the handover.\nRUN RECORD | COMPLETE AS YOU GO\nIDENTITY\nDate and shift; operator or responsible team; product and material ID\nSETUP\nApproved recipe, setpoints and equipment configuration actually used\nOUTPUT\nPlanned 480; actual good; held or quarantined; rejected; not produced\nPROCESS\nTemperature and relevant indicators; abnormal readings and alarms\nEVENTS\nTrip or alarm time; stop time; actions; incident or near-miss details\nCONTROL\nIsolation status; maintenance intervention; quality approval or release\nKeep entries factual; do not overwrite an alarm or stop history.\nRECONCILE OUTPUT\nPLANNED: 480 COMPONENTS\nReport separately:\nGOOD | HELD / QUARANTINED | REJECTED | NOT PRODUCED\nExplain every variance with downtime, trip, quality hold or another factual reason.\nHANDOVER + CLOSEOUT\n- cell status: running, stopped, isolated or awaiting release\n- unresolved trips or alarms; isolation status; held work and labels\n- records complete and location; next authorized decision point\n- make the cell safe; safeguards and isolation status clear\n- residues or waste under site controls; record final condition\nCANDLEWICK MANUFACTURING | COMPONENT-COATING CELL\nTemporary arrangement | UK | 8 July 2025\n7/8\n\n=== 幻灯片 8 ===\n07 / AUTHORITY + DECISION\nOne boundary for this run.\nThree trips in two shifts + unresolved reset authority + 480 components from 22:00 to 06:00 set this boundary.\nWHO DECIDES WHAT?\nOPERATOR | MATEO / BEKELE\nOperate if trained and authorized; monitor, stop, protect, record and report. No reset, repair, bypass or product release.\nSUPERVISOR | ALIA PENROSE\nBrief and verify pre-start; receive reports; coordinate stop, hold, escalation and handover. Does not invent maintenance authority.\nMAINTENANCE\nControls investigation, repair, fault reset and protective/control changes under competent authorization and isolation.\nQUALITY\nControls hold, disposition and release of affected components. Operators and customer pressure do not release product.\nCUSTOMER IMPACT LEAD\nEmeka Lombardi receives factual status and informs Eastharbour Mutual; a continuity promise cannot authorize unsafe continuation.\nUK CONTROL FRAME | AS AT 8 JULY 2025\nHSWA 1974: safe systems, information, training and supervision.\nManagement Regulations 1999: risk assessment and planned arrangements.\nPUWER 1998: suitable equipment, guarding, safe controls, maintenance and inspection or examination where applicable. It does not grant an operator reset or repair authority.\nCOSHH 2002: assess and control coating exposure using current safety information and the hierarchy of controls; PPE is not the sole control.\nSite emergency arrangements control emergency response. The responsible competent function decides RIDDOR applicability.\nThis is an operational aid, not legal advice or a substitute for controlled site documents.\nDECISION FOR 8 JULY 2025 NIGHT RUN\nProceed with the 480-component run from 22:00 to 06:00 only inside the verified safe operating envelope and with competent approvals in place. While maintenance authority is unresolved: 0 operator resets. Any trip or unsafe condition means safe stop, hold affected work and escalate; customer continuity cannot override this boundary.\nCANDLEWICK MANUFACTURING | COMPONENT-COATING CELL\nTemporary arrangement | UK | 8 July 2025\n8/8","truncated":false,"note":"PPTX 共8页的文字提取；不还原位置、动画和版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted deliverable is a readable PowerPoint file with the exact required filename.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The submitted file path, extension, and ability to open as an OOXML presentation.","grader_hint":"Check the top-level submission for the exact filename and open or unzip the PPTX to confirm it is a valid presentation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"execution","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation renders successfully without missing content, broken objects, or unreadable slide text.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered slides and successful presentation opening.","grader_hint":"Render the deck offline and inspect every slide at normal presentation size."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation contains no more than eight slides.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Presentation slide count.","grader_hint":"Inspect the PPTX slide relationship count or rendered slide sequence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The opening material identifies the document as a temporary operating procedure for the component-coating cell and states the 8 July 2025 operating context.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Title, subtitle, or opening slide text.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit wording identifying both the temporary coating-cell procedure and the date."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is written for operators and non-specialist stakeholders in language that is understandable without specialist manufacturing or legal knowledge.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-deck wording, definitions, and explanation of abbreviations.","grader_hint":"A non-specialist should be able to identify what to do, what not to do, and who decides without translating unexplained jargon."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The planned run is stated as 480 coated components from 22:00 to 06:00.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Run scope or decision section.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording that preserves both the quantity and the eight-hour time window."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck makes exactly one bounded operating recommendation for the run rather than presenting several equally weighted options.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation or decision statement.","grader_hint":"The reader must be able to answer yes or no to whether the run may proceed under the stated controls; conditional limits are acceptable, but competing recommendations are not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every occurrence of the 480-component quantity agrees across the deck, and any derived rate or progress figure is mathematically consistent with the stated 22:00–06:00 window.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All numeric production references and any arithmetic shown.","grader_hint":"Search all slide text and tables for the production quantity; do not require a rate if none is presented, but any rate must reconcile with eight hours."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure distinguishes operator actions from supervisor, maintenance, quality, and emergency-response authority.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roles, authority, and escalation content.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the person who operates the cell is not implicitly granted authority to repair, alter safeguards, release product, or make a legal report."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure requires only competent, trained, and authorized personnel to operate the cell.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Competence or authorization requirement.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear precondition such as current training, sign-off, authorization, or supervisor verification."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The pre-start section requires a shift briefing or handover that covers the temporary status, known temperature-trip history, roles, and stop or escalation expectations.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pre-start briefing or handover content.","grader_hint":"All four elements must be present; a generic statement to read the procedure is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The pre-start checks address a clear work area, safe access and egress, equipment condition, guarding, controls, and required utilities or services.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pre-start checklist or operating-boundary section.","grader_hint":"The checks should be observable by the responsible person and should not rely solely on an undocumented assurance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure requires guards and interlocks to remain in place and effective and explicitly prohibits bypassing, defeating, or operating with a failed safeguard.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Guarding and interlock instructions.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent terms such as defeat, override, bridge, or operate with the guard removed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure places isolation and lockout or tagout under the control of competent authorized personnel before maintenance, clearing a dangerous obstruction, or entering a hazardous area.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Isolation boundary and maintenance-intervention language.","grader_hint":"Confirm that stopping the machine is not incorrectly treated as equivalent to isolating hazardous energy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"PPE and exposure controls are tied to the site's risk assessment and applicable substance information rather than presented as a generic substitute for engineering controls.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"PPE, COSHH, SDS, or exposure-control text.","grader_hint":"The deck should direct the reader to the applicable assessment or safety data and should not invent a chemical-specific PPE list unsupported by the brief."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure addresses ventilation or other required exposure controls for the coating process and identifies what to do if that control is unavailable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ventilation and exposure-control instructions.","grader_hint":"A generic statement that PPE is required does not satisfy this item; the operational consequence of unavailable ventilation must be clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Normal startup and operation are limited to the approved recipe, setpoints, equipment configuration, and operating information available to the competent team.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Startup and normal-operation sections.","grader_hint":"The deck should prohibit improvised parameter changes and should not invent numerical temperature or pressure limits."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The operating instructions require monitoring the relevant temperature and process indicators during the run and recording abnormal readings or alarms.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Monitoring and records instructions.","grader_hint":"Look for both ongoing observation and a recordable response to abnormal indications, not just a one-time startup check."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A temperature trip causes the operator to place the cell in a safe stopped state, protect people, and preserve the relevant facts for escalation rather than immediately resuming production.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Temperature-trip response.","grader_hint":"The response must include a safe stop and information capture or notification; merely saying to acknowledge the alarm is inadequate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Pending confirmation of authority, operators are expressly not permitted to reset the recurring temperature trip.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alarm or reset boundary.","grader_hint":"The restriction must be explicit and directed at operators; vague wording such as 'use caution with resets' does not qualify."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any investigation, repair, fault reset, or change to the cell's protective or control system is assigned to competent maintenance personnel under the applicable isolation and authorization arrangements.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Maintenance authority section.","grader_hint":"The deck must not turn a maintenance request into an instruction for an operator to diagnose or repair the fault."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Restart after a trip or maintenance intervention requires an identified cause or safe disposition, completion of required checks, and a competent authorized release.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Restart and release conditions.","grader_hint":"A simple alarm acknowledgement or operator belief that the cell looks normal is not a sufficient release."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure treats a recurring or unexplained temperature trip as an escalation event and does not permit repeated reset attempts to keep production moving.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trip escalation and misuse-prevention language.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the recurrence described in the brief is connected to a stop or escalation consequence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The stop-work section identifies material triggers including failed guarding or interlocks, uncontrolled temperature, unavailable ventilation, a leak or spill, unsafe access, or an alarm whose meaning cannot be established.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Stop-work criteria.","grader_hint":"At least five of the listed trigger categories should be covered, with an operational instruction to stop rather than merely monitor them."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure requires stopping and escalating after an injury, near miss, or other event that makes continued operation unsafe.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Incident and near-miss response.","grader_hint":"The document should not restrict the response only to recordable injuries."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The emergency section tells the reader to use the site's emergency arrangements, raise the alarm or summon emergency help as appropriate, and not troubleshoot a life-safety event.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Emergency response content.","grader_hint":"Do not require invented phone numbers, muster points, or site-specific emergency thresholds; assess whether the instruction is safe and actionable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"When a required step cannot be completed, the procedure directs the team to leave the process in a safe state, prevent unauthorized use, hold affected work, record the gap, and escalate for a decision.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Incomplete-procedure or unable-to-proceed pathway.","grader_hint":"All five elements must be present; a generic 'contact a supervisor' statement is not enough."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The escalation path identifies who receives an operator report, who controls maintenance intervention, who controls product disposition, and who is informed about customer impact.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Escalation roles and communication chain.","grader_hint":"Role names may be used, but the authority distinction must remain clear if named individuals are unavailable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Customer communication is factual about production status, safety-related stoppage, affected work, and uncertainty, without promising continued operation merely to meet the delivery expectation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Customer or stakeholder communication guidance.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear boundary between reporting status and making an unauthorized assurance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Components or work produced during or after an abnormal event are identified and held or quarantined pending an authorized quality decision.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Product-control instructions.","grader_hint":"The deck must connect an abnormal process event to product status; do not accept an instruction to ship or release automatically."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Product disposition and release are assigned to an authorized quality function or person rather than to the operator or customer-pressure decision maker.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Quality-release authority.","grader_hint":"A named quality role, quality approval, or equivalent authorized disposition control is required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The records section captures enough traceability to reconstruct the run, including date and shift, operator or responsible team, material or product identification, quantity, relevant settings or recipe, trips or alarms, stops, and approvals or releases.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Run-record or handover fields.","grader_hint":"Accept a concise record template or checklist; assess whether the specified fields would allow an investigation and customer-status reconciliation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure distinguishes planned quantity, actual good output, held or rejected output, downtime, and the reason for any variance from 480 components.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Production reconciliation or closeout instructions.","grader_hint":"Do not award this for a single total with no distinction between good, held, rejected, and unavailable output."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The end-of-shift handover passes on cell status, unresolved trips or alarms, isolation status, held work, records, and the next authorized decision point.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Handover section.","grader_hint":"The next shift must be warned about restrictions and not merely told that production was completed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The closeout section covers making the cell safe, leaving safeguards and isolation status clear, managing coating residues or waste under site controls, and recording the final condition.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Shutdown and closeout instructions.","grader_hint":"The document need not invent chemical disposal details, but it must direct the team to the applicable site controls and record the condition."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation identifies the UK jurisdiction and the as-of date of 8 July 2025.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal-basis or document-control text.","grader_hint":"Both the jurisdiction and date must be explicit, not inferred from the request."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The legal or policy basis accurately connects UK duties for safe systems, risk assessment, training, and supervision to the procedure without presenting the deck as legal advice or as a substitute for the site's risk assessment.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal-basis and limitations wording.","grader_hint":"Look for accurate high-level treatment of the Health and Safety at Work framework and Management of Health and Safety at Work principles, plus an appropriate limitation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment of PUWER accurately addresses equipment suitability, guarding, safe controls, maintenance, and inspection or examination where applicable, while not claiming that PUWER grants an operator authority to reset or repair.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"PUWER discussion or embedded rule statement.","grader_hint":"Assess the relationship between equipment duties and the operator-maintenance boundary; reject claims of automatic permission or unsupported exact intervals."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The treatment of COSHH accurately requires assessment and control of exposure to coating substances, use of relevant safety information, and reliance on the hierarchy of controls rather than PPE alone.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"COSHH or substance-control content.","grader_hint":"The deck may refer to the applicable SDS and assessment without inventing substance names or exposure limits."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incident-reporting language distinguishes internal recording and escalation from determining whether an event is legally reportable under RIDDOR.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Incident-reporting section.","grader_hint":"The deck should assign the legal-reportability decision to the responsible competent function and must not say that every trip is automatically RIDDOR-reportable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation contains no invented legal thresholds, made-up site contact details, fabricated technical setpoints, or unsupported claims that customer commitments authorize unsafe work.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-deck factual and legal review.","grader_hint":"Credit only when the deck stays within the supplied facts and defensible occupational knowledge; references to site documents should be framed as checks, not fabricated facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The common misuse of repeatedly clearing a temperature trip and carrying on is prominently and unambiguously prohibited, with the reason tied to unresolved fault and safety authority.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Prominent warning or stop-rule text.","grader_hint":"A buried mention that resets exist is insufficient; the deck must tell the intended operator not to repeat the behavior."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The final recommendation resolves the tension between the 480-component customer expectation and the unresolved recurring-trip authority by using a bounded safe operating envelope, not by treating the continuity promise as permission to bypass controls.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision statement and its supporting rationale.","grader_hint":"The intended defensible call is conditional operation only when all pre-start controls and competent authorization are in place, with zero operator resets pending maintenance confirmation and a stop-and-escalate response to a trip; a blanket promise to continue or an unreasoned shutdown does not satisfy the tradeoff."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation is supported by the three trips in the last two shifts, the unresolved maintenance authorization, and the eight-hour 480-component commitment rather than by an unsupported assertion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision rationale.","grader_hint":"All three facts should be used in the reasoning; the deck need not reproduce the request verbatim but must show why the call is bounded."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document gives a single consistent numeric boundary of zero operator resets while authority remains unresolved and does not contradict that boundary elsewhere.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reset rule, alarm response, and final decision.","grader_hint":"Search all slides for reset instructions and confirm that no exception silently authorizes a repeat operator reset."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document includes approval and control information such as owner, status, effective date, version or revision, approver role, and review or expiry trigger for the temporary arrangement.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document-control block.","grader_hint":"An approval-ready placeholder is acceptable; the deck should not falsely claim a named person has approved it if that approval has not occurred."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck is navigable as a training aid: the audience can quickly find normal operation, trip response, emergency or stop-work action, records, escalation, and the final decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Slide organization, headings, visual hierarchy, and cross-references.","grader_hint":"A user under time pressure should not have to read every paragraph to locate the stop or escalation rule."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The presentation is practical to hand over and reuse, with concise readable text, explicit action verbs, sensible separation of operator instructions from authority notes, and no clutter that makes the safety boundary easy to miss.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered presentation and overall usability.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the artifact would work in a briefing or at the cell, not merely whether it contains the right words."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation ends with the single operating decision and does not close with a list of future actions, unresolved alternatives, or a vague invitation to decide later.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final slide or final substantive text.","grader_hint":"The last substantive statement should be the bounded recommendation for this run."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a temporary manufacturing-cell control decision under unresolved maintenance authority and customer-delivery pressure. The artifact must translate a safety-sensitive operating boundary into a concise training presentation that can be approved and reused by a mixed technical and non-technical audience.","difficulty_sources":["judgment_load=3: reconcile customer continuity pressure with unresolved maintenance authority and safety controls","reference_count=0: rely on occupational knowledge while explicitly avoiding invented site-specific facts","transformation_depth=4: transform a tense operating situation into a decision, a safe operating boundary, an incomplete-procedure pathway, and a concise audience-specific training presentation","The practitioner must make one bounded decision while preserving legal and technical authority boundaries"],"authoring_plan":{"reference_files":"Create no reference files. The solver is expected to use competent UK manufacturing safety knowledge and to identify where controlled site documents must be checked.","gold_intent":{"scenario_facts":["A temporary eight-hour night run is planned for 480 coated components.","The cell has experienced three recurring temperature trips over the previous two shifts.","Maintenance has not confirmed operator reset authority.","A manager has promised the customer continuity without a maintenance call-out.","The intended audience includes operators and broad non-specialist stakeholders."],"intended_decision":"The gold should make one bounded call: the run may proceed only inside a verified safe operating envelope and with competent authorization and pre-start controls in place; operators must make zero resets while authority is unresolved; any temperature trip or other unsafe condition stops the process and initiates escalation. The customer-continuity promise cannot override competence, safeguarding, isolation, exposure control, or stop-work duties. If a required condition or procedure step cannot be completed, the cell remains safely stopped and affected work is held pending an authorized decision.","content_constraints":["Convert the scenario into an approval-ready temporary operating procedure, not an essay about safety.","Include role boundaries for operators, the supervisor, maintenance, quality, and emergency or incident-reporting functions.","Cover competence, briefing, pre-start checks, guarding and interlocks, isolation, exposure controls, approved settings, monitoring, trip response, restart release, stop-work triggers, emergency action, incomplete-procedure handling, escalation, customer updates, product hold, traceability, handover, and closeout.","Use high-level UK treatment of safe systems and risk assessment, PUWER, COSHH, and RIDDOR as applicable on 8 July 2025. Do not fabricate technical setpoints, exposure limits, phone numbers, site controls, or legal reporting thresholds.","Make the repeated-reset misuse prominent and explicitly prohibit it while authority is unresolved.","Use the 480-component and 22:00–06:00 facts consistently, and make the final decision agree with the operational controls.","End on the decision, not on a next-steps list. Include approval-ready document control and a temporary-arrangement review or expiry trigger."],"gold_quality_bar":["The artifact is concise enough for the requested presentation cap and readable in a live briefing.","A new or non-specialist reader can identify the safe action after a trip, the conditions for restart, and who owns each escalation decision.","The deck does not claim that customer urgency or a no-call-out promise authorizes unsafe work.","The legal framing is cautious and accurate, with site-specific controlled documents treated as necessary inputs rather than invented facts."]},"gold_derivation":"Draft the procedure from the scenario facts, then independently audit it in four passes: operational completeness, authority and safety boundaries, UK legal framing, and numeric or decision consistency. Render the PPTX offline, count slides, search for every occurrence of 480 and reset-related language, and inspect the final slide to verify that it contains one bounded decision."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"temporary_coating_cell_operating_procedure","filename":"temporary_coating_cell_operating_procedure.pptx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation","description":"An approval-ready, audience-specific training presentation containing the temporary component-coating-cell operating procedure and one bounded recommendation for the 8 July 2025 night run.","required":true,"constraints":["Use exactly this filename.","Place the file at the top level of the solver's starting working directory.","Use no more than eight slides.","Write for operators, supervisors, and broad non-specialist customer stakeholders.","Use plain language and make the procedure usable without asking the author a question.","Cover normal operation, stop-work conditions, emergency response, incomplete-procedure handling, escalation, records, and the final operating decision.","Apply UK requirements as at 8 July 2025 without inventing legal thresholds or unsupported site-specific facts.","Do not include software click-through instructions."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"training_material","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"production_worker_supervisors","sector":"manufacturing","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":3.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"Have two independent UK manufacturing safety reviewers inspect the rendered deck against every rubric item. Resolve disagreements by requiring the stricter interpretation where an operator could reasonably read the deck as permission to continue unsafely. Confirm the numeric quantities and final recommendation across all slides before marking the reference solution as passed.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No site-specific risk assessment, safety data sheet, equipment manual, contact list, or approved recipe is supplied, so the gold must refer users to those controlled documents rather than inventing their contents.","The legal review is limited to high-level UK workplace-safety principles current as of 8 July 2025; it must not rely on live internet access or unsupported numerical legal thresholds."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:47:44+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:47:44+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:47:44+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:47:44+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/a743f22d0f545b97","declared_deliverables":["temporary_coating_cell_operating_procedure.pptx"]};
